  "world": "\u2b50\u2b50\u2b50 W-MC \u2014 THE ZONE WIDTH IS A CONSTANT IN THE CODE. A synthetic NORMAL world at n=968 and B=1200, containing no skew and no arm-to-arm variation whatsoever, reproduces sd(r) = 0.027699 against the observed 0.027341, a ratio of 1.0131; the asymptotic quantile-error derivation independently gives 0.027821. Two routes, one answer. \u2b50 And sd(r) falls with the resample count at a fitted log-log slope of -0.4951, which is the -0.5 of Monte-Carlo error. \u2b50\u2b50 SO R726's DISAGREEMENT ZONE IS SET BY NBOOT, NOT BY THE ARMS: raising the resample count shrinks it, and 'the spread would have to double' describes a choice made in R294:117 rather than a property of the data. \u26a0 The skew question is NOT answered, it is DISSOLVED: r and the asymmetry are functionals of the same resamples, and the zero-skew simulation already produces |corr| = 0.0202 with no skew present, against an observed 0.1342 \u2014 indistinguishable at 3 simulation SDs -- and that comparison is UNDERPOWERED by this round's own CORR-POWER control, so it acquits nothing. On the statistic the positive control PROVES can see planted skew, the observed mean asymmetry is -0.00410 with SE 0.00296: z = -1.38 against zero skew and z = -9.43 against the planted lognormal world, so these cells carry no detectable skew by a powered test. \u26a0 And n cannot be tested here at all: it takes two values with 4 of 82 cells at the minority level. That is an unidentified covariate, reported as unidentified rather than as a null.",
  "z_vs_planted_skew": -9.43376256539063,
  "z_vs_zero_skew": -1.3844429995222438
}